From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SplitPlay <splitplay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 12:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] splitplay: inject input on its own thread under MMCSS "Games"

Moonlight's input packets were injected on Sunshine's single task_pool
thread, which also runs unrelated tasks (session timeouts, the force-quit
timer, deferred work), so patch 0012 left it out of MMCSS: it ran at the
process priority class, and an input event could wait behind any other
pool task.

Input now has a pool of its own, input_pool, with exactly one thread
(input::init() starts it, the input deinit stops it after running what is
still queued and before the backend is released). The thread registers
with MMCSS "Games" at normal priority, like the capture, encode, send and
control threads, and is named input::inject.

Everything that touches the injected input state moves with it, so the
state keeps upstream's synchronisation (one thread, no new locks) and its
order: the control thread still appends packets to the session's input
queue and posts one passthrough_next_message per packet (FIFO, batching
unchanged), and key repeat, the delayed left button, the Home emulation,
input::reset(), gamepad removal, the ViGEm rumble and LED callbacks, the
DualShock 4 report timer and touch and pen repeat are queued on the same
thread. Relative mouse moves, gamepad state and rumble go through the same
code as before, only on this thread. task_pool keeps the other tasks.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01J21Rm42dakZVfEnnyh7aQn

